#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Discord, as an agent is reached on it.

Everything about this platform is in this file and nowhere else. A snowflake, an intent bit, the
two-thousand-character cap, the close codes that mean *stop trying* — none of them appears anywhere
under `src/rundesk/`, nothing there imports `discord.py`, and nothing here imports rundesk. The
seam is a pipe carrying newline-delimited JSON, and it is what lets a product whose own code
imports nothing outside the standard library reach a platform that can only be reached over a
websocket.

**A channel is a connection, not a place.** One bot per agent. It answers private messages and
every room it has been invited to, with nothing per-place configured anywhere, and one list of
ids — `RUNDESK_ALLOW` — governs both. There is no room to look up and paste, no server id to go and
find, and no second `--check` that would say anything different. The build this replaces wrote a
channel per *kind of place*, which is a shape for governing a public room full of strangers, and
strangers are not what this is for.

**Named in a room, the exchange moves into a thread, and goes on there without being named again.**
A room is somebody else's, and the reason is stronger than tidiness: *a reply that rewrites itself
in place is unreadable, so only a running commentary may grow* — an answer that arrives whole, at
the end, after minutes of working, needs somewhere of its own to arrive into. So the message that
names us opens a thread, the answer goes in there, and inside a thread this bot opened it answers
with nobody having to name it a second time. A private message stays flat, because there is nobody
to be out of the way of. A message in a room that neither names us nor stands in a thread of ours
is not answered at all: an agent in a shared server is quiet until it is spoken to.


Three invocations, sharing almost nothing:

    --capabilities   what this can do — offline, no account, the same answer every time
    --check          sign in, and report what was found, so a person confirms rather than pastes
    serve            hold the connection open and carry conversations until rundesk ends us

## What crosses the seam

Records go out on **stdout**, one JSON object per line, flushed as it happens. Commands come in on
**stdin**, the same way. **stderr is for what has no words of ours** — a traceback — and goes to a
file the gateway rotates. Nothing else may go to stdout: it is a protocol, and one traceback
written across it is a line no reader can parse in the middle of a stream it is parsing. Something
worth an owner's attention that *does* have words is a `note` record, which is a line a reader can
parse.

## What is rundesk's and is never decided here

**Rundesk decides what state a turn is in; this decides only how that looks** — a mark, a typing
indicator, and nothing else. An adapter working out on its own when a message had been seen would
be re-implementing the turn, and two surfaces would eventually disagree about one run.

**Whether somebody may be answered is rundesk's.** `RUNDESK_ALLOW` is read here only to avoid
working for nothing: reacting, or fetching anything, for somebody who can never be answered is
waste at best and, in a room full of people, an agent visibly attending to a stranger it is about
to ignore. A message from anybody else is dropped in silence, because replying to a stranger to
tell them they are a stranger confirms the agent is listening and spends the owner's tokens doing
it.

**Splitting is rundesk's**, in one place, for every platform. The text handed to `deliver` is
already inside `MAX_TEXT` and is never re-split here. The build this replaces put the limit in each
adapter, and the consequence is on the record: Slack fixed a splitting bug and Discord still had
the original.

**Which interpreter runs this is rundesk's.** It arrives on `PATH` — see `channels/adapters.py`.
The build this replaces had each adapter count parent directories to find the install's virtualenv;
the count was wrong for a whole release and nothing failed until somebody added a channel. So
`discord.py` is imported by name, lazily, and is never located.
"""

