#: The vendor library, bound by `the_library()` at the moment it is first needed.
#:
#: **Never imported at module scope**, because `--capabilities` must answer on a machine where
#: nothing is installed: it is asked before a channel exists, offline, and an adapter that cannot
#: say what it can do until its dependency is present is one rundesk cannot ask.
discord = None

#: What Discord takes in one message from a bot. **The four-thousand-character Nitro limit does not
#: apply to us**, and believing it produces a message the platform refuses outright. Nothing here
#: splits at this number — it is *checked*, because a text longer than this is rundesk having failed
#: to split, and cutting it quietly would report a delivery as whole that was not.
MAX_TEXT = 2000

#: What this can do, asked with no network and no account, and the same answer every time. Read when
#: a channel is added and written into its record, so that a surface which *cannot* edit a message
#: is told apart from one that can and did not.
#:
#: `thread` says this opens one when it is named in a room, and that the exchange then goes on
#: inside it as an ordinary conversation, reached and identified by that thread's own id. **Rundesk
#: is never told about a place it has not been given the id of**: the thread is reported as the
#: `conversation` on the very record that opened it, so nothing here moves a turn somewhere the
#: other side of the seam could not address.
#:
#: `max_text` is the constant above and never a second copy of it. Two copies of one limit is how
#: the build this replaces came to have a splitting rule fixed on one platform and not the other.
CAPABILITIES = {"stream": True, "edit": "full", "react": True, "thread": True, "attach": True,
                "max_text": MAX_TEXT}

#: What Discord is told this connection wants:
#:
#:     GUILDS                  1 <<  0   what the rooms and threads of a server are
#:     GUILD_MESSAGES          1 <<  9   a room's messages
#:     GUILD_MESSAGE_REACTIONS 1 << 10   what becomes of the marks this program puts up
#:     DIRECT_MESSAGES         1 << 12   a private conversation, content and all
#:     MESSAGE_CONTENT         1 << 15   what a room's messages actually say — **privileged**
#:
#: **`MESSAGE_CONTENT` is privileged and is here anyway, and this is the one trade in this file.**
#: Without it Discord sends the content of a direct message and of any guild message that @-mentions
#: this bot, and blanks everything else — including every message inside a thread this bot opened
#: itself. So the two halves of what a room is for are one decision and not two: a thread can be
#: opened without it, and then nothing said in that thread can be read unless the person types the
#: bot's name again on every line, which reads as a feature that is broken rather than one that is
#: absent. It costs one toggle in the Developer Portal — *Message Content Intent*, on the Bot page —
#: and, past a hundred servers, Discord's own verification. `--check` reads the application's flags
#: and refuses while it is off, because the failure it prevents is `4014`: not resumable, exit `78`,
#: and from the outside a bot that simply never came up.
#:
#: **`GUILDS` is here because without it a thread cannot be told from a room, and no thread can be
#: opened at all.** With nothing cached, discord.py hands every guild message a `PartialMessageable`
#: that knows only an id: `isinstance(channel, Thread)` is false inside a thread, `message.guild` is
#: `None`, and `Message.create_thread` refuses outright with *"this message does not have guild info
#: attached"*. It is not privileged — nobody has to toggle anything for it. What it costs is the
#: room and role cache of every server this bot is in, which is the library's and is proportional to
#: servers joined.
#:
#: **`GUILD_MEMBERS` and `GUILD_PRESENCES` are still deliberately absent.** Nothing here reads a
#: member list or a presence, and each is a second toggle bought for nothing.
#:
#: Nothing here reads a reaction. The reactions intent is declared because what a gateway is told at
#: identify is fixed for the life of that connection, and because it is not privileged.
INTENTS = 38401

#: What the invite asks a server for, one named permission at a time so that a reader can check each
#: against something this file actually does. **Short on purpose**: a permission granted for nothing
#: is a permission somebody was asked for under false pretences. There is no `CREATE_PRIVATE_THREADS`
